path exists, but it is membership-level eviction, not per-source resolution. Creator issues `acl` revoke of the held author, closes, adopts, then issues `acl` grant and closes again (existing public `issue()` surface, existing creator authority). The returning author gets a new `admissionEpoch` (design.md ACL transition law; test `sameKeyReentry` asserts `[writer.author, 4, null]` at tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1448). On the next reopen its old rows classify `old-incarnation` (packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:4944-4955) and are skipped by the outbox (v3-live.ts:7044-7058) because `openProgressSources` only ever contains entries carrying `replacementProgress` (v3-live.ts:8822-8826) and a `manual-review` entry can never carry progress (packages/issuance-store/src/contract.ts:457). `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rebuilds entries only from the returned sources (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2931-2953), so the held entry is dropped at merge, the prune gate releases (packages/issuance-store/src/maintenance.ts:113-124), the plan becomes empty and the fence issues (RED case 7 `F5B_C07_EMPTY_PLAN_STILL_FENCES`). The store permits the entry to disappear: `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` (contract.ts:546-593) constrains only progress-bearing or already-linked entries. Limits that make it unsuitable as case 11's promised resolver: it is author-wide (every held entry dies at once, reviewed or not), it discards the held content (recoverable only by the application re-issuing it as fresh operations, as the design already licenses), it requires creator ACL authority plus two closes, it is user-visible membership churn (kick + re-invite), and it restores only the roles the operator remembers to re-grant. It resolves the plan by voiding it, not by dispositioning the reviewed source.\",\"recommended_option\":\"C_DEFER_RESOLUTION\",\"new_api_required\":false,\"reasoning\":\"The Sol RED finding is confirmed on all three legs. (1) `manualReviewHold` proves only persistence: it asserts a durable unfenced plan, an unchanged lineage and an unchanged boundary while the creator advances (tests/…-integration-red.test.ts:1505-1554); nothing resolves anything. (2) `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rejects a changed disposition with a bare `TypeError(\\\"v3 room settlement plan source differs\\\")` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941-2943), and that throw propagates through `rebasePromise` into `terminalFailure` + `shutdown()` (index.ts:3538-3546), so an operator who edits policy after review fails the session closed instead of resolving it. (3) `V3RoomSession` (index.ts:414-431) exposes no resolver, and the only caller of `releaseSettlementHold` is `closeSession()` (index.ts:4319), which exists so shutdown can proceed. Because `drainSettlementOutbox` awaits the hold inside the lifetime-transition queue (index.ts:3158-3163, 3529-3547) and is never re-entered, no in-session resolution is even reachable today. Deeper than the reported finding: the hold is not a fail-closed refusal, it is an unresolvable pending promise. `issue()` awaits `rebasePromise` (index.ts:4405), so a held author's `issue()` never settles; RED cases 11 and 21 assert `rejects.toThrow()` (tests:1522, 1385-1388) on promises that can only settle when something else closes the session. Those two fixtures therefore cannot go GREEN under current hold semantics regardless of which resolution option is chosen — this is an independent blocker for parent f5b GREEN. `sealEpoch()` also awaits `rebasePromise` (index.ts:4467), so a manual-review entry belonging to the creator — the only closing authority under `creator-trusted-settlement-v1`, and explicitly subject to the same plan/fence path — freezes every author's boundary, contradicting the matrix row and `F5B_C11_ONLY_HELD_AUTHOR_STALLS`, which only ever exercises a non-creator writer. On option choice: A is not a small addition. A per-source resolver needs a read surface (list held sources with their exact operations) plus a write surface, and it cannot be called at all while the hold blocks the lifetime-transition queue, so it requires converting the hold from a never-settling await into a resumable state machine — a public-API and control-flow change next to fail-closed settlement, which the plan's own precedent (production-hardening-tdd-plan-v2.md:100318-100320, 100331-100338) says must stop for a separately reviewed high-risk slice. B is genuinely cheap — the application already supplies a fresh `displacementPolicies` table on every reopen (the `displacedControls` fixture does exactly that at tests:1369-1384), and the store already permits the CAS rewrite, so B is a narrow relaxation of one room guard — but it is action-granular, not per-source (index.ts:2915 keys policy by `action`), so in a room where every held item is a `message` it flips every held source at once. Calling that \\\"resolution after human review\\\" would be dishonest about what the operator actually approved, and it would remove the only guard currently standing in for a store-side transition law. C is therefore correct for f5b: preserve `manual-review` as an indefinite fail-closed hold, amend case 11 to state what is actually demonstrable, and move resolution to a named later slice. Nothing in the construction itself is unsound — the fence/plan/checkpoint semantics, contiguity, prune gate and one-fence-per-epoch bound all hold up under this audit — so the reslice is confined to case 11's obligation and the hold's liveness semantics, not to the settlement design. One transparency note: this session has no shell, so I could not recompute the SHA-256 digests in `manifest.sha256`; I verified only that the manifest names exactly the three files present in the checkpoint directory, and that `pre-review.md` independently records the design digest as matching the plan record.\",\"golden_path_assessment\":\"Discord/MMORPG operator UX today is unusable and unsafe, independent of which option is chosen. A held author's client silently hangs on `issue()` with no typed error and no way to enumerate what is held; the only recovery is `close()`. Nothing surfaces the held operations to a moderator, so \\\"manual review\\\" has no review surface. If the held author is the creator, the shard stops closing epochs entirely. Per-source decisions: the existing eviction path is author-wide; B is action-wide; only A gives true per-source granularity, and only if it also exposes the held content for inspection. Restart durability is the one strong dimension: the hold lives in the durable plan and is re-derived from durable truth at every reopen, so it survives crash, close and cold reopen unchanged. Replay/idempotence is also sound for all options: the plan entry is the idempotence key, one-way disposition plus the `replacementSequence` link plus the fence precondition (contract.ts:643-652) already prevent double apply and redisposition, so a resolver that CASes a disposition and then issues one replacement inherits existing protection. Authority is unchanged in every option: displacement policy is declared local recovery behavior, not consensus validity (plan record ~:50426), and every replacement is a fresh vertex signed by the author's own key and authorized normally, so neither B nor A grants any new authority. ACL changes interact well and badly at once — removal + re-add already clears holds implicitly, which is the existing path, but it means any explicit resolver must treat an already-dropped entry as an idempotent no-op rather than an error. Same-key devices are the sharpest UX trap: plans are per-issuance-scope, so resolving on device A leaves device B's own displaced rows held; a per-source API must be documented as per-device, or operators will believe a resolution is global. Offline recovery is fine for all options (purely local at reopen, no coordination). Bounded custody/pruning is correct today and must not regress: `manual-review` always holds the prune gate (maintenance.ts:124), and the gate must release exactly when the entry is dropped or linked. Compatibility and API/schema/wire impact: C is zero, B changes no type and no schema (one room guard), A adds two public `V3RoomSession` members and no wire/protobuf/crypto/threshold change; `creator-trusted-v1` is untouched in all three because the entire settlement path is profile-gated. Redisposition risk is low for one-way transitions and unacceptable for two-way: the store currently has no backstop against `rebase -> manual-review` after a durable fence.\",\"security_contract\":\"Invariants any option must preserve, and which are already enforced where. No fence while any `manual-review` entry remains: enforced in the store at contract.ts:643-646 and in the room at index.ts:3160-3163 — a resolver may only run while `fenceSequence === null`, which is automatic since the hold blocks the fence. No linked entry is ever re-dispositioned: contract.ts:651 refuses a replacement effect on a linked or `manual-review` entry, and `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` freezes progress-bearing entries. Never change a linked entry, never mutate a source row, never touch `sourceDigest`: a resolution must carry the exact `(sourceSequence, sourceDigest)` it reviewed and fail closed on mismatch, so a stale operator decision cannot land on a different row after a merge. Transitions must be one-way `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` only; the reverse must be refused, because a rewrite to `manual-review` after a durable fence permanently re-arms the prune gate and makes the entry unlinkable. That law is currently enforced only by the room's stricter equality guard (index.ts:2941) and has no store-side backstop — whichever option is adopted, the law belongs in `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition`, not in the room. Custody: the prune gate must keep refusing while an entry is unlinked and non-`expire`; a resolution to `expire` releases the gate and must therefore be as deliberate as a delete. Authority: resolution authority is the local application's own policy over its own device-local plan, signing with the author's own key — no new authority carrier, no forged authority, no cross-author effect, and no test-store mutation is ever admissible as authority. Ambiguous outcomes: a resolution CAS must be idempotent under restart, and an entry already dropped at merge (boundary settled it, or re-admission voided it) must resolve to an explicit \\\"already settled\\\" outcome rather than a session-killing throw. Non-negotiables confirmed untouched by every option considered: no wire/protobuf, cryptography, dependency or threshold change, and `creator-trusted-v1` stays byte-identical because the settlement path is gated behind `settlementProfileFor`.\",\"required_slice\":\"Named prerequisite before parent f5b GREEN: **D.110c-0c1f5b0w — settlement manual-review hold semantics** (f5b0v is taken; f5b0t/f5b0u/f5b0v are GREEN-closed). Tests-first RED then GREEN, scoped to exactly four things and nothing else. (1) Convert the hold from a never-settling await into a typed fail-closed refusal: `issue()` under a held plan must reject with a stable settlement-held error instead of blocking on `rebasePromise`, which is what makes RED cases 11 and 21 satisfiable at all. (2) Decouple the close path from a held drain: `sealEpoch()` must not wait on this author's own `manual-review` hold, so a held creator still closes and only its own boundary stalls — consistent with matrix rows 15/16, which already permit replacements after a close. (3) Move the one-way disposition law into `@ts-drp/issuance-store` (`assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition`), refusing any `X -> manual-review` rewrite and any `sourceDigest` change on an unlinked entry, with conformance vectors; this is a backstop, not a behavior change, since the room is stricter today. (4) Amend design.md case 11, the matrix `manual-review entry` row, and the plan record to state the demonstrable truth. Because item 1 corrects a public session-call semantic next to fail-closed settlement, it takes the same bounded multi-model plan review the plan record already requires for public-contract corrections (~:100322-100327). Separately, and only if the product commits to per-source operator review: **D.110c-0c1f5b0x — per-source settlement resolution API**, a separately reviewed high-risk slice, not a prerequisite for parent f5b GREEN. Its minimum semantic boundary, stated here so it is not improvised later: input identity is the exact `(objectId, author, sourceSequence, sourceDigest)` plus one target disposition; a read surface must first expose held entries with their exact detached operations, since resolving unseen content is not review. Authority is the local application over its own device-local plan only — no cross-author, no cross-device effect. Allowable transitions are `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` only, once, while `fenceSequence === null` and `replacementSequence === null` and no `replacementProgress` exists. Idempotence is CAS on `revision` with an explicit already-resolved / entry-absent outcome rather than a throw. It may run only after the drain has held and before the fence; it must re-enter the drain rather than require a reopen, which is why item 1 above (resumable hold) is its true prerequisite. Errors are typed refusals that never set `terminalFailure`. Interaction: it never touches a linked entry, never affects the fence rule, and releases the prune gate exactly when the entry is dropped or linked. Restart re-derives everything from the durable plan; multi-device semantics are explicitly per-device and must be documented as such.\",\"parent_f5b_disposition\":\"Parent f5b GREEN stays blocked, and no resolver is added to it. Under the currently authorized coverage-corrective tests-only RED, amend RED case 11 to what is demonstrable and honest: \\\"Manual-review hold: no plan completion, no fence, no replacements; other authors advance; the hold is durable across restart, close and cold reopen; `issue()` fails closed for the held author; the plan completes only when the source leaves the plan at merge — by boundary settlement or by authenticated re-admission under a new `admissionEpoch` — with per-source resolution owned by D.110c-0c1f5b0x.\\\" Amend the matrix row `manual-review entry` the same way and add the creator carve-out from f5b0w item 2 so \\\"only that author stalls\\\" is true rather than aspirational. Add one honest line to the \\\"Retained findings absorbed\\\" list recording that per-source manual-review resolution is deferred with a named owner, matching the design's own P2 disposition rule. The parent RED's `manualReviewHold` and `displacedControls` fixtures keep their current assertions and gain: an assertion that the held `issue()` rejects with the typed settlement-held error (satisfiable only after f5b0w), an assertion that the creator still closes while a non-creator is held, and — as the honest demonstration that the plan can complete — a revoke/close/adopt/grant/close/adopt/reopen leg showing the held entry dropped at merge, the prune gate released and the empty plan fenced. That leg uses only existing authenticated product surfaces, no direct store mutation, no forged authority and no new API. Parent GREEN must not touch `writeMergedSettlementPlan`'s disposition guard, must not add any session or application member, and must not relax the fence, prune or entry-immutability rules; every one of those is f5b0w or f5b0x scope and naming it here is what keeps it from being silent expansion.\",\"findings\":[{\"severity\":\"P1\",\"title\":\"Case 11's resolver does not exist and is unreachable in-session\",\"evidence\":\"design.md:501 promises \\\"resolving the hold later completes the plan\\\". `settlementHold` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2005-2007) is resolved only by `closeSession()` (:4319), which exists so shutdown can proceed. `drainSettlementOutbox` awaits it inside the lifetime-transition queue (:3158-3163, :3529-3547) and is never re-entered, `V3RoomSession` (:414-431) exposes no resolver, and `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rejects a changed disposition outright (:2941-2943). The parent RED `manualReviewHold` (tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1505-1554) asserts only durable persistence.\",\"action\":\"Adopt option C: amend design case 11 and the matrix row to the demonstrable statement, and name D.110c-0c1f5b0x as the owner of per-source resolution.\"},{\"severity\":\"P1\",\"title\":\"Held issue() hangs instead of failing closed, so RED cases 11 and 21 cannot go GREEN\",\"evidence\":\"`issue()` awaits `rebasePromise` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:4405); under a hold that promise never settles (:3529-3547 -> :3160-3162). Both fixtures assert `rejects.toThrow()` on it — tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1522 (`F5B_C11_MANUAL_REVIEW_BARRIER`) and :1385-1388 (`F5B_C21_DISPLACED_ACL_HOLDS_BEFORE_FENCE`) — so the call can only settle if something else closes the session; otherwise the fixture deadlocks to the 60s timeout.\",\"action\":\"f5b0w item 1: replace the unresolvable await with a typed settlement-held refusal at `issue()`, keeping the hold itself indefinite and fail-closed.\"},{\"severity\":\"P1\",\"title\":\"A creator-owned manual-review entry stalls every author, not just its own\",\"evidence\":\"`sealEpoch()` awaits `rebasePromise` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:4467) and the creator is the only closing authority under `creator-trusted-settlement-v1`; design.md states the creator's own displaced rows take the same plan/fence path (:181-182) while the matrix row (:391) and `F5B_C11_ONLY_HELD_AUTHOR_STALLS` claim only that author stalls — the fixture only ever holds a non-creator writer (tests:1505-1538).\",\"action\":\"f5b0w item 2: decouple the close path from this author's own held drain (matrix rows 15/16 already permit replacements after a close), or state the restriction honestly in the matrix.\"},{\"severity\":\"P2\",\"title\":\"Store contract has no one-way disposition law; only the room guard stands in for it\",\"evidence\":\"`assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` (packages/issuance-store/src/contract.ts:546-593) constrains only progress-bearing or already-linked entries, and contract.ts:457 forbids progress on `manual-review` entries, so no branch constrains them. A CAS write may flip `rebase -> manual-review` after `fenceSequence` is durable, permanently re-arming the prune gate (packages/issuance-store/src/maintenance.ts:113-124) and making the entry unlinkable (contract.ts:651). Only examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941 prevents this today — the exact guard option B would relax.\",\"action\":\"f5b0w item 3: enforce `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` one-way, with `sourceDigest` immutability, in the store with conformance vectors.\"},{\"severity\":\"P2\",\"title\":\"A legitimate policy change fails the session closed rather than refusing typed\",\"evidence\":\"examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941-2943 throws a bare `TypeError(\\\"v3 room settlement plan source differs\\\")`; the throw is caught at :3538-3546, assigned to `terminalFailure` and triggers `shutdown()`. An operator who edits `displacementPolicies` after review therefore bricks that author's session instead of being refused.\",\"action\":\"Whichever option is adopted, make the merge emit a typed refusal (or an explicitly allowed transition); never a session-terminal throw.\"},{\"severity\":\"P2\",\"title\":\"Option B is action-granular, not per-source\",\"evidence\":\"`settlementDisposition` looks policy up by `action` only (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2915, and again at :3119, :3381, :3442); `displacementPolicies` is `Readonly<Record<string, ...>>` (:360). In a room whose held items are all `message`, one policy flip resolves every held source at once.\",\"action\":\"Do not describe B as per-source review; if the product needs per-source decisions, that is D.110c-0c1f5b0x with a held-source read surface.\"},{\"severity\":\"P2\",\"title\":\"Migration redirect forces manual-review on every source and would hang session creation\",\"evidence\":\"`settlementDisposition` returns `manual-review` whenever `redirectSource !== undefined` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2927), and the redirect constructor awaits `rebasePromise` (:4153-4160). A migration target carrying any displaced source with intents would never return from `createV3RoomSessionOwned` rather than fail closed. Latent today because the target scope is fresh, but it is the same defect class as the held `issue()` hang.\",\"action\":\"Cover the redirect path in f5b0w item 1 so migration-import debt fails closed with a typed error instead of hanging.\"},{\"severity\":\"P2\",\"title\":\"Design manifest hashes were not recomputed in this session\",\"evidence\":\".logs/d110c-0c1f5b0r-design-3a156aca/manifest.sha256 lists design.md, pre-review.md and next-prompt.md; all three exist. No shell tool is available in this session, so the SHA-256 values could not be recomputed. The only corroboration is pre-review.md:3-5, which records the design digest as matching both the manifest and the plan record.\",\"action\":\"Recompute the three digests in a shell-capable session before treating the checkpoint as byte-verified at b7751f72.\"}]}","structured_output":{"verdict":"STOP_AND_RESLICE","existing_resolution_path":"One genuine, authorized path exists, but it is membership-level eviction, not per-source resolution. Creator issues `acl` revoke of the held author, closes, adopts, then issues `acl` grant and closes again (existing public `issue()` surface, existing creator authority). The returning author gets a new `admissionEpoch` (design.md ACL transition law; test `sameKeyReentry` asserts `[writer.author, 4, null]` at tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1448). On the next reopen its old rows classify `old-incarnation` (packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:4944-4955) and are skipped by the outbox (v3-live.ts:7044-7058) because `openProgressSources` only ever contains entries carrying `replacementProgress` (v3-live.ts:8822-8826) and a `manual-review` entry can never carry progress (packages/issuance-store/src/contract.ts:457). `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rebuilds entries only from the returned sources (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2931-2953), so the held entry is dropped at merge, the prune gate releases (packages/issuance-store/src/maintenance.ts:113-124), the plan becomes empty and the fence issues (RED case 7 `F5B_C07_EMPTY_PLAN_STILL_FENCES`). The store permits the entry to disappear: `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` (contract.ts:546-593) constrains only progress-bearing or already-linked entries. Limits that make it unsuitable as case 11's promised resolver: it is author-wide (every held entry dies at once, reviewed or not), it discards the held content (recoverable only by the application re-issuing it as fresh operations, as the design already licenses), it requires creator ACL authority plus two closes, it is user-visible membership churn (kick + re-invite), and it restores only the roles the operator remembers to re-grant. It resolves the plan by voiding it, not by dispositioning the reviewed source.","recommended_option":"C_DEFER_RESOLUTION","new_api_required":false,"reasoning":"The Sol RED finding is confirmed on all three legs. (1) `manualReviewHold` proves only persistence: it asserts a durable unfenced plan, an unchanged lineage and an unchanged boundary while the creator advances (tests/…-integration-red.test.ts:1505-1554); nothing resolves anything. (2) `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rejects a changed disposition with a bare `TypeError(\"v3 room settlement plan source differs\")` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941-2943), and that throw propagates through `rebasePromise` into `terminalFailure` + `shutdown()` (index.ts:3538-3546), so an operator who edits policy after review fails the session closed instead of resolving it. (3) `V3RoomSession` (index.ts:414-431) exposes no resolver, and the only caller of `releaseSettlementHold` is `closeSession()` (index.ts:4319), which exists so shutdown can proceed. Because `drainSettlementOutbox` awaits the hold inside the lifetime-transition queue (index.ts:3158-3163, 3529-3547) and is never re-entered, no in-session resolution is even reachable today. Deeper than the reported finding: the hold is not a fail-closed refusal, it is an unresolvable pending promise. `issue()` awaits `rebasePromise` (index.ts:4405), so a held author's `issue()` never settles; RED cases 11 and 21 assert `rejects.toThrow()` (tests:1522, 1385-1388) on promises that can only settle when something else closes the session. Those two fixtures therefore cannot go GREEN under current hold semantics regardless of which resolution option is chosen — this is an independent blocker for parent f5b GREEN. `sealEpoch()` also awaits `rebasePromise` (index.ts:4467), so a manual-review entry belonging to the creator — the only closing authority under `creator-trusted-settlement-v1`, and explicitly subject to the same plan/fence path — freezes every author's boundary, contradicting the matrix row and `F5B_C11_ONLY_HELD_AUTHOR_STALLS`, which only ever exercises a non-creator writer. On option choice: A is not a small addition. A per-source resolver needs a read surface (list held sources with their exact operations) plus a write surface, and it cannot be called at all while the hold blocks the lifetime-transition queue, so it requires converting the hold from a never-settling await into a resumable state machine — a public-API and control-flow change next to fail-closed settlement, which the plan's own precedent (production-hardening-tdd-plan-v2.md:100318-100320, 100331-100338) says must stop for a separately reviewed high-risk slice. B is genuinely cheap — the application already supplies a fresh `displacementPolicies` table on every reopen (the `displacedControls` fixture does exactly that at tests:1369-1384), and the store already permits the CAS rewrite, so B is a narrow relaxation of one room guard — but it is action-granular, not per-source (index.ts:2915 keys policy by `action`), so in a room where every held item is a `message` it flips every held source at once. Calling that \"resolution after human review\" would be dishonest about what the operator actually approved, and it would remove the only guard currently standing in for a store-side transition law. C is therefore correct for f5b: preserve `manual-review` as an indefinite fail-closed hold, amend case 11 to state what is actually demonstrable, and move resolution to a named later slice. Nothing in the construction itself is unsound — the fence/plan/checkpoint semantics, contiguity, prune gate and one-fence-per-epoch bound all hold up under this audit — so the reslice is confined to case 11's obligation and the hold's liveness semantics, not to the settlement design. One transparency note: this session has no shell, so I could not recompute the SHA-256 digests in `manifest.sha256`; I verified only that the manifest names exactly the three files present in the checkpoint directory, and that `pre-review.md` independently records the design digest as matching the plan record.","golden_path_assessment":"Discord/MMORPG operator UX today is unusable and unsafe, independent of which option is chosen. A held author's client silently hangs on `issue()` with no typed error and no way to enumerate what is held; the only recovery is `close()`. Nothing surfaces the held operations to a moderator, so \"manual review\" has no review surface. If the held author is the creator, the shard stops closing epochs entirely. Per-source decisions: the existing eviction path is author-wide; B is action-wide; only A gives true per-source granularity, and only if it also exposes the held content for inspection. Restart durability is the one strong dimension: the hold lives in the durable plan and is re-derived from durable truth at every reopen, so it survives crash, close and cold reopen unchanged. Replay/idempotence is also sound for all options: the plan entry is the idempotence key, one-way disposition plus the `replacementSequence` link plus the fence precondition (contract.ts:643-652) already prevent double apply and redisposition, so a resolver that CASes a disposition and then issues one replacement inherits existing protection. Authority is unchanged in every option: displacement policy is declared local recovery behavior, not consensus validity (plan record ~:50426), and every replacement is a fresh vertex signed by the author's own key and authorized normally, so neither B nor A grants any new authority. ACL changes interact well and badly at once — removal + re-add already clears holds implicitly, which is the existing path, but it means any explicit resolver must treat an already-dropped entry as an idempotent no-op rather than an error. Same-key devices are the sharpest UX trap: plans are per-issuance-scope, so resolving on device A leaves device B's own displaced rows held; a per-source API must be documented as per-device, or operators will believe a resolution is global. Offline recovery is fine for all options (purely local at reopen, no coordination). Bounded custody/pruning is correct today and must not regress: `manual-review` always holds the prune gate (maintenance.ts:124), and the gate must release exactly when the entry is dropped or linked. Compatibility and API/schema/wire impact: C is zero, B changes no type and no schema (one room guard), A adds two public `V3RoomSession` members and no wire/protobuf/crypto/threshold change; `creator-trusted-v1` is untouched in all three because the entire settlement path is profile-gated. Redisposition risk is low for one-way transitions and unacceptable for two-way: the store currently has no backstop against `rebase -> manual-review` after a durable fence.","security_contract":"Invariants any option must preserve, and which are already enforced where. No fence while any `manual-review` entry remains: enforced in the store at contract.ts:643-646 and in the room at index.ts:3160-3163 — a resolver may only run while `fenceSequence === null`, which is automatic since the hold blocks the fence. No linked entry is ever re-dispositioned: contract.ts:651 refuses a replacement effect on a linked or `manual-review` entry, and `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` freezes progress-bearing entries. Never change a linked entry, never mutate a source row, never touch `sourceDigest`: a resolution must carry the exact `(sourceSequence, sourceDigest)` it reviewed and fail closed on mismatch, so a stale operator decision cannot land on a different row after a merge. Transitions must be one-way `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` only; the reverse must be refused, because a rewrite to `manual-review` after a durable fence permanently re-arms the prune gate and makes the entry unlinkable. That law is currently enforced only by the room's stricter equality guard (index.ts:2941) and has no store-side backstop — whichever option is adopted, the law belongs in `assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition`, not in the room. Custody: the prune gate must keep refusing while an entry is unlinked and non-`expire`; a resolution to `expire` releases the gate and must therefore be as deliberate as a delete. Authority: resolution authority is the local application's own policy over its own device-local plan, signing with the author's own key — no new authority carrier, no forged authority, no cross-author effect, and no test-store mutation is ever admissible as authority. Ambiguous outcomes: a resolution CAS must be idempotent under restart, and an entry already dropped at merge (boundary settled it, or re-admission voided it) must resolve to an explicit \"already settled\" outcome rather than a session-killing throw. Non-negotiables confirmed untouched by every option considered: no wire/protobuf, cryptography, dependency or threshold change, and `creator-trusted-v1` stays byte-identical because the settlement path is gated behind `settlementProfileFor`.","required_slice":"Named prerequisite before parent f5b GREEN: **D.110c-0c1f5b0w — settlement manual-review hold semantics** (f5b0v is taken; f5b0t/f5b0u/f5b0v are GREEN-closed). Tests-first RED then GREEN, scoped to exactly four things and nothing else. (1) Convert the hold from a never-settling await into a typed fail-closed refusal: `issue()` under a held plan must reject with a stable settlement-held error instead of blocking on `rebasePromise`, which is what makes RED cases 11 and 21 satisfiable at all. (2) Decouple the close path from a held drain: `sealEpoch()` must not wait on this author's own `manual-review` hold, so a held creator still closes and only its own boundary stalls — consistent with matrix rows 15/16, which already permit replacements after a close. (3) Move the one-way disposition law into `@ts-drp/issuance-store` (`assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition`), refusing any `X -> manual-review` rewrite and any `sourceDigest` change on an unlinked entry, with conformance vectors; this is a backstop, not a behavior change, since the room is stricter today. (4) Amend design.md case 11, the matrix `manual-review entry` row, and the plan record to state the demonstrable truth. Because item 1 corrects a public session-call semantic next to fail-closed settlement, it takes the same bounded multi-model plan review the plan record already requires for public-contract corrections (~:100322-100327). Separately, and only if the product commits to per-source operator review: **D.110c-0c1f5b0x — per-source settlement resolution API**, a separately reviewed high-risk slice, not a prerequisite for parent f5b GREEN. Its minimum semantic boundary, stated here so it is not improvised later: input identity is the exact `(objectId, author, sourceSequence, sourceDigest)` plus one target disposition; a read surface must first expose held entries with their exact detached operations, since resolving unseen content is not review. Authority is the local application over its own device-local plan only — no cross-author, no cross-device effect. Allowable transitions are `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` only, once, while `fenceSequence === null` and `replacementSequence === null` and no `replacementProgress` exists. Idempotence is CAS on `revision` with an explicit already-resolved / entry-absent outcome rather than a throw. It may run only after the drain has held and before the fence; it must re-enter the drain rather than require a reopen, which is why item 1 above (resumable hold) is its true prerequisite. Errors are typed refusals that never set `terminalFailure`. Interaction: it never touches a linked entry, never affects the fence rule, and releases the prune gate exactly when the entry is dropped or linked. Restart re-derives everything from the durable plan; multi-device semantics are explicitly per-device and must be documented as such.","parent_f5b_disposition":"Parent f5b GREEN stays blocked, and no resolver is added to it. Under the currently authorized coverage-corrective tests-only RED, amend RED case 11 to what is demonstrable and honest: \"Manual-review hold: no plan completion, no fence, no replacements; other authors advance; the hold is durable across restart, close and cold reopen; `issue()` fails closed for the held author; the plan completes only when the source leaves the plan at merge — by boundary settlement or by authenticated re-admission under a new `admissionEpoch` — with per-source resolution owned by D.110c-0c1f5b0x.\" Amend the matrix row `manual-review entry` the same way and add the creator carve-out from f5b0w item 2 so \"only that author stalls\" is true rather than aspirational. Add one honest line to the \"Retained findings absorbed\" list recording that per-source manual-review resolution is deferred with a named owner, matching the design's own P2 disposition rule. The parent RED's `manualReviewHold` and `displacedControls` fixtures keep their current assertions and gain: an assertion that the held `issue()` rejects with the typed settlement-held error (satisfiable only after f5b0w), an assertion that the creator still closes while a non-creator is held, and — as the honest demonstration that the plan can complete — a revoke/close/adopt/grant/close/adopt/reopen leg showing the held entry dropped at merge, the prune gate released and the empty plan fenced. That leg uses only existing authenticated product surfaces, no direct store mutation, no forged authority and no new API. Parent GREEN must not touch `writeMergedSettlementPlan`'s disposition guard, must not add any session or application member, and must not relax the fence, prune or entry-immutability rules; every one of those is f5b0w or f5b0x scope and naming it here is what keeps it from being silent expansion.","findings":[{"severity":"P1","title":"Case 11's resolver does not exist and is unreachable in-session","evidence":"design.md:501 promises \"resolving the hold later completes the plan\". `settlementHold` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2005-2007) is resolved only by `closeSession()` (:4319), which exists so shutdown can proceed. `drainSettlementOutbox` awaits it inside the lifetime-transition queue (:3158-3163, :3529-3547) and is never re-entered, `V3RoomSession` (:414-431) exposes no resolver, and `writeMergedSettlementPlan` rejects a changed disposition outright (:2941-2943). The parent RED `manualReviewHold` (tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1505-1554) asserts only durable persistence.","action":"Adopt option C: amend design case 11 and the matrix row to the demonstrable statement, and name D.110c-0c1f5b0x as the owner of per-source resolution."},{"severity":"P1","title":"Held issue() hangs instead of failing closed, so RED cases 11 and 21 cannot go GREEN","evidence":"`issue()` awaits `rebasePromise` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:4405); under a hold that promise never settles (:3529-3547 -> :3160-3162). Both fixtures assert `rejects.toThrow()` on it — tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b-integration-red.test.ts:1522 (`F5B_C11_MANUAL_REVIEW_BARRIER`) and :1385-1388 (`F5B_C21_DISPLACED_ACL_HOLDS_BEFORE_FENCE`) — so the call can only settle if something else closes the session; otherwise the fixture deadlocks to the 60s timeout.","action":"f5b0w item 1: replace the unresolvable await with a typed settlement-held refusal at `issue()`, keeping the hold itself indefinite and fail-closed."},{"severity":"P1","title":"A creator-owned manual-review entry stalls every author, not just its own","evidence":"`sealEpoch()` awaits `rebasePromise` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:4467) and the creator is the only closing authority under `creator-trusted-settlement-v1`; design.md states the creator's own displaced rows take the same plan/fence path (:181-182) while the matrix row (:391) and `F5B_C11_ONLY_HELD_AUTHOR_STALLS` claim only that author stalls — the fixture only ever holds a non-creator writer (tests:1505-1538).","action":"f5b0w item 2: decouple the close path from this author's own held drain (matrix rows 15/16 already permit replacements after a close), or state the restriction honestly in the matrix."},{"severity":"P2","title":"Store contract has no one-way disposition law; only the room guard stands in for it","evidence":"`assertSettlementPlanProgressTransition` (packages/issuance-store/src/contract.ts:546-593) constrains only progress-bearing or already-linked entries, and contract.ts:457 forbids progress on `manual-review` entries, so no branch constrains them. A CAS write may flip `rebase -> manual-review` after `fenceSequence` is durable, permanently re-arming the prune gate (packages/issuance-store/src/maintenance.ts:113-124) and making the entry unlinkable (contract.ts:651). Only examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941 prevents this today — the exact guard option B would relax.","action":"f5b0w item 3: enforce `manual-review -> expire|rebase|transform` one-way, with `sourceDigest` immutability, in the store with conformance vectors."},{"severity":"P2","title":"A legitimate policy change fails the session closed rather than refusing typed","evidence":"examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2941-2943 throws a bare `TypeError(\"v3 room settlement plan source differs\")`; the throw is caught at :3538-3546, assigned to `terminalFailure` and triggers `shutdown()`. An operator who edits `displacementPolicies` after review therefore bricks that author's session instead of being refused.","action":"Whichever option is adopted, make the merge emit a typed refusal (or an explicitly allowed transition); never a session-terminal throw."},{"severity":"P2","title":"Option B is action-granular, not per-source","evidence":"`settlementDisposition` looks policy up by `action` only (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2915, and again at :3119, :3381, :3442); `displacementPolicies` is `Readonly<Record<string, ...>>` (:360). In a room whose held items are all `message`, one policy flip resolves every held source at once.","action":"Do not describe B as per-source review; if the product needs per-source decisions, that is D.110c-0c1f5b0x with a held-source read surface."},{"severity":"P2","title":"Migration redirect forces manual-review on every source and would hang session creation","evidence":"`settlementDisposition` returns `manual-review` whenever `redirectSource !== undefined` (examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2927), and the redirect constructor awaits `rebasePromise` (:4153-4160). A migration target carrying any displaced source with intents would never return from `createV3RoomSessionOwned` rather than fail closed. Latent today because the target scope is fresh, but it is the same defect class as the held `issue()` hang.","action":"Cover the redirect path in f5b0w item 1 so migration-import debt fails closed with a typed error instead of hanging."},{"severity":"P2","title":"Design manifest hashes were not recomputed in this session","evidence":".logs/d110c-0c1f5b0r-design-3a156aca/manifest.sha256 lists design.md, pre-review.md and next-prompt.md; all three exist. No shell tool is available in this session, so the SHA-256 values could not be recomputed. The only corroboration is pre-review.md:3-5, which records the design digest as matching both the manifest and the plan record.","action":"Recompute the three digests in a shell-capable session before treating the checkpoint as byte-verified at b7751f72."}]},"ttft_ms":2381,"type":"result","duration_ms":479441,"uuid":"cf251d0e-8dd7-44da-b384-07381c13a890","ttft_stream_ms":1947,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":1948,"queued_turn_count":0}
