eader (:5) maps \"5,11: manualReviewHold\", and `manualReviewHold` (:1505-1554) only exercises the `manual-review` sub-case (`F5B_C05_C11_NO_FENCE_OR_REPLACEMENT_WHILE_HELD`, :1524). Design case 5 (`design.md:487-489`) requires all three refusals: missing plan, `manual-review`, and `fenceSequence` already set. The other two ARE covered by the closed f5b0s slice — tests/phase-6b-d110c-0c1f5b0s-settlement-plan-red.test.ts:408 (\"refuses a fence when the durable plan is absent\") and :418 (\"refuses a fence when fenceSequence is already set\") — but unlike cases 18/26/27 and the causalJoin classifier (test :11-19, :1414-1418), the header does not cite them by file and exact title.","impact":"A reviewer reading the ownership map cannot tell whether the two uncited sub-cases are covered by a closed prerequisite or simply missing; the record explicitly requires every case to be either exercised here or attributed to a closed owner.","required_action":"Owner: the test's parent case-ownership comment block. Disposition: add the two exact f5b0s titles to the header map for case 5, matching the citation style already used for cases 18/26/27; no new executable coverage is needed."}],"causal_red_assessment":"The accepted RED is genuinely causal. `.logs/d110c-0c1f5b-red-cecde972/result.json` records one execution, runner status 1, two selected tests in one file, 1 failed / 1 passed, zero skipped/todo, zero top-level errors, no additional soft failures. The sole failure is `F5B_SETTLEMENT_PROFILE_SUCCESSOR_CODEC_REQUIRED: genuine settlement successor fails CERTIFIED_VALUE_MISMATCH before checkpoint production`, thrown at test :684 from the `close()` helper at :1672 after 417.58 ms.\n\nThe chain is real product, not a missing import/export or setup error. `openRoom(2)` builds a genuine genesis invite with `profileId: \"creator-trusted-settlement-v1\"` and a version-3 latched ACL (accepted today: packages/protocol-v3/src/latched-acl.ts:145 allows 256 members at version 3, :254 gates version 3 on `settlementProfileFor(...) === \"v1\"`). The test performs real issue, real publication through the transport, real creator ingress with awaited admission acks, and a genuine published displaced two-intent batch (`F5B_REAL_BATCH_SOURCE`, :1666) before calling `sealEpoch()`. The failure originates in production: packages/protocol-v3/src/creator-close.ts:276 (`prepareCreatorAnchorSigningRequest`) requires the literal `creator-trusted-v1`, which the seal actor maps to `CERTIFIED_VALUE_MISMATCH`, and packages/node/src/creator-close.ts:1031 throws `creator close actor failed: CERTIFIED_VALUE_MISMATCH`. The helper catches only that exact string and rethrows the labelled token; it substitutes no profile, checkpoint, signature, actor result or authority.\n\nThe five hard-coded profile sites the test pins all exist as claimed and are genuine (not synthetic-success adapters): creator-close.ts:276 (`profile.profileId !== \"creator-trusted-v1\"`), :541 (`profileId: \"creator-trusted-v1\"` in `completeCreatorSuccessor`), :598 (`decodedRecord.profileId !== \"creator-trusted-v1\"` in `openCreatorSuccessorTrust`), creator-checkpoint.ts:144 (`decoded.profileId !== ...` in `trustRecord`) and :242 (reconstructed genesis record). All five source-attribution assertions ran to completion before the throw, per the reporter timing and both rejected-run assessments.\n\nNo forged record, substituted checkpoint or fixture-only trust exists. `checkpoint()` (:704-748) reads the real proposed closure captured from `inspectCreatorTransitionAdvance` and opens it with the production codec `openCreatorAuthorSettlement` under `advance.successorTrust` as `floorTrust`; the codec's `OPEN_KEYS` (packages/protocol-v3/src/creator-author-issuance-frontiers.ts:43-51) matches the seven keys the test passes exactly, and it independently re-verifies the ed25519 signature and every binding. `F5B_C26_FLOOR_ONLY_CHECKPOINT_OPEN` and `F5B_NO_LEGACY_CONTROLS` are real product oracles. The v1 control also observed a genuine production string, `v3 room successor reopen failed: D110C_FLOOR_MISMATCH: creator successor differs from the authenticated room-head floor`, which matches packages/node/src/creator-adoption-activate.ts:447/:502 wrapped by examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:2362.","coverage_assessment":"The continuation covers the parent-owned matrix substantially better than the superseded `3a9e9329` attempt, and the two prior Sol P1s (missing parent composition cases; inadequate 64-writer oracles) are materially closed.\n\nVerified present and correctly constructed: case 1 (`F5B_C01_DELAYED_DEPENDENCY_NOT_ADMITTED`, :1674); 2/3/10 (fence-precedes-replacement at :1688, committed-prefix crash at :1683, unlinked-suffix-once at :1722); 4/15/16/19 in `delayedPublication` for all three modes, including `F5B_C16_NEXT_EPOCH_LARGER_FENCE` and `F5B_C15_DISPLACED_REPLACEMENT_BECOMES_SOURCE`; 5/11 manual-review hold with a durable unfenced plan and a proof that only the held author stalls (:1533-1538); 6-9 same-key reentry across three absent closes, new incarnation `[author, 4, null]`, never-reset device sequence, empty-plan-still-fences, fresh-device fence at slot 0, and a real stale-envelope ingress rejection; 12/23 adversarial creator fence scan; 13 negative (:1704-1709) and positive; 14 three transitions plus cold reopen; 20/21 displaced controls; 22 genesis incarnation; 24a/24c; 25; 26 in-test plus f5b0a.\n\nThe adversarial fence scan is genuinely sound: `makeFence(10_002, 10_003)` is rejected at ingress by production authentication (packages/protocol-v3/src/index.ts:3533-3539 refuses a fence whose `fenceSequence > authorSequence`), so the contiguous scan legitimately stops at 10_001 rather than crossing into slot 10_002 — the expectation matches design steps 2 and 4 exactly.\n\nNon-duplicated cases are cited to closed owners and every citation checks out: cases 18/26/27 to tests/d110c-0c1f5b0a-settlement-codec-red.test.ts:540, :491 and :524, and interrupted pre-floor adoption to tests/phase-6b-d110c-0b0a-staged-handoff-red.test.ts:162 and :246 — all four titles exist verbatim.\n\nGaps: case 17's `creator-trusted-v1` half is ungated (P2), and case 5's absent-plan/already-fenced refusals are covered by closed f5b0s tests but not cited in the ownership map (P2). Checkpoint-derived reachability for `openProgressSources` is genuinely required by the fixture: `F5B_AUTHENTICATED_SOURCE_IS_TERMINAL` (:1694) places the source at or below `terminalThrough`, so the later `F5B_OPEN_PROGRESS_SOURCES_AUTHENTICATED_FRONTIER_REACHABILITY` (:1715) can only pass if `openProgressSources` (packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:8822-8827, consumed at :7054) is actually consulted — the frontier's stated obligation is met, not evaded.","realm_assessment":"The per-peer `creator-adoption-activate` query-isolated import is a faithful model of independent physical clients, and I found no bypass or false-positive route.\n\nThe guard being isolated is genuinely module-local: `activeOwners` is a module-scope `Map` at packages/node/src/creator-adoption-activate.ts:73, read at :361 and written at :403/:426, with the \"creator successor already has a conflicting active owner\" refusal at :364. In production, independent devices are independent processes with independent module state, so one realm per physical peer is the correct model — the rejected run `1dcff170` proved the un-isolated fixture collided precisely there, and `8fcbc039` proved the isolation resolves it (the v1 control then advanced past the first non-creator reopen).\n\nProduction source is preserved: the mock imports `../packages/node/src/creator-adoption-activate.ts` with a `?f5bClient=<peerId>` query — the same unedited source file, not a rewrite. Vite does not propagate arbitrary queries to a module's own imports, so the realm's `import \"./creator-adoption.js\"` and `./internal/creator-adoption-intent.js` resolve to the single shared instances; opaque intent custody, handle aliasing and recovery dependencies keep exact identity, as the mock's comment claims.\n\nThe export surface is fully covered with no leak: `creator-adoption-activate.ts` exports exactly `activateCreatorSuccessorAdoption` (:435) and `reopenCreatorSuccessorAdoption` (:457), and examples/v3-room/src/index.ts:8-11 imports exactly those two. Both are wrapped by `capture`, so there is no path where ownership is registered in realm X but released through the shared module. Release stays correct because the wrapped `deactivate` closure installed at :250-263 is captured inside the owning realm, so `room.close()` deletes from the right map.\n\nOpaque capability identity is preserved — `capture` passes the caller's original input object through unchanged and returns the real result; no handle, capability, checkpoint, floor or activation result is substituted. Same-peer restart ownership is preserved because `realms` is keyed by `peerId` and never cleared or reset, so a peer keeps one realm across every close/reopen; the singleton guard therefore still operates within each client. The transport-identity assertion at :126-128 pins `peerId` to the exact `d110c-f5b-parent-<n>-peer-<n>(-fresh)?` shape, and the cross-realm identity check at :117-120 positively proves distinct function identities per peer.\n\nResidual notes, not findings: the isolation necessarily removes any cross-client `navigator.locks` dimension (the rejected assessment states this honestly — \"not a claim of separate OS processes, independent browser globals, or a cross-window Web Lock test\"), and no assertion positively demonstrates that a same-peer double activation is still refused within one realm — the guard is preserved structurally but not exercised.","golden_path_assessment":"The 64-writer composition is a genuine one-room product golden path, not a membership count.\n\nOne real room: `openRoom(64)` builds a single objectId with one version-3 latched ACL carrying 64 ACL-authorized members (index 0 admin/finality/writer, the rest